 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_mimosa",
   "title": "The Mimosa, 1865",
   "text": "On 28 May 1865 a converted tea clipper, the Mimosa, sailed from Liverpool with about 150 Welsh men, women and children. They landed on 28 July at what became Puerto Madryn, Porth Madryn to them. The day is still kept in Chubut as G\u0175yl y Glaniad, the Landing Festival."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_why",
   "title": "Why Patagonia?",
   "text": "The settlers wanted a place where Welsh could be the language of chapel, school, court and home, safe from the pressure on it in Wales. Michael D. Jones, a minister from Bala, championed the idea. Argentina, keen to settle the south, offered land in the Chubut valley."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_canals",
   "title": "Water in a Dry Land",
   "text": "The first years were hard: drought, failed harvests and floods. The settlers learnt to dig canals from the Chubut river to water the valley, and wheat from Y Wladfa later won prizes abroad. The poplars you see were planted as windbreaks against the Patagonian wind."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_tehuelche",
   "title": "The Tehuelche",
   "text": "The land was not empty. The Tehuelche people knew it, traded with the settlers and taught them to hunt guanaco and rhea. Their own story has a darker chapter: the Argentine army's Conquest of the Desert campaigns of the 1870s and 1880s took their land."
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_railway",
   "title": "The Old Station",
   "text": "The museum is in Gaiman's old railway station. The Central Chubut Railway was built in the 1880s to carry the valley's grain to the port, partly by Welsh workers who came out on the ship Vesta in 1886. The trains have long gone."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_first_house",
   "title": "T\u0177 Cyntaf",
   "text": "The first house built in Gaiman, in 1874, by David Roberts. It is small, plain and built to last, like most things here. Gaiman itself takes its name from a Tehuelche word."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_trelew",
   "title": "Trelew and Rawson",
   "text": "Down the valley, Trelew is named after Lewis Jones, one of the colony's leaders: tre means town, so Tre-Lew is Lewis's town. Rawson, the capital of Chubut, is named after Guillermo Rawson, the Argentine minister who backed the settlement."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_eisteddfod",
   "title": "Eisteddfod y Wladfa",
   "text": "Y Wladfa holds its own eisteddfod every year, with competitions in Welsh and Spanish: singing, poetry, recitation and folk dance. Winners take a chair or a crown, as in Wales. Choirs from Wales come out to compete, and sometimes go home beaten."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_cwm_hyfryd",
   "title": "Cwm Hyfryd",
   "text": "In the 1880s Welsh riflemen rode west across Patagonia to the Andes and found a green valley they called Cwm Hyfryd, the pleasant valley. In 1902, when Argentina and Chile settled their border, the Welsh there voted to stay in Argentina. Trevelin, the mill town, is still there."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "abroad_gaiman_today",
   "title": "Welsh Today",
   "text": "A few thousand people in Chubut speak some Welsh. Since 1997 teachers from Wales have come out each year to teach it, and there are bilingual Welsh and Spanish schools in Gaiman and Trelew. Most people here are Argentines first, with a Welsh grandmother and a very good recipe."
  }
 ],
